INT. DRIVE-IN PROJECTION BOOTH - TWILIGHT

A cramped booth over an empty lot. Film canisters. A coffee can of cigarette butts. Through the small window, rows of silent cars waiting in the dusk.

PEGGY LOUISE WALDROP, 17, in a pressed uniform, threads a reel with practiced fingers while her younger brother TUCKER WALDROP, 12, sits on a stool kicking his heels, a comic book on his knee. Between them, MR. ABERNATHY, 60s, the theater owner, in a short-sleeved shirt and bow tie, checks his pocket watch, taps the projector's housing, and sets a thermos by the door.

MR. ABERNATHY
Seven forty. Start her at eight sharp.

PEGGY
Yes, sir.

MR. ABERNATHY
And Peggy, no repeats of last week.

PEGGY
It wasn't my fault, sir. The reel was upside down.

MR. ABERNATHY
Mm.

He nods, pats Tucker on the head, and leaves. His footsteps fade down the iron stairs. Tucker turns a page. Peggy finishes lacing the film and flips a switch. The lamp hums warm.

TUCKER
Is he gone?

PEGGY
He's gone.

TUCKER
Good.

He slides the comic book aside and pulls a rumpled envelope from his shirt. Hands it up without looking at her. Peggy takes it. Turns it over. Her name in his careful block capitals.

PEGGY
What's this?

TUCKER
Open it.

She does. Inside, a ticket stub and a folded strip of paper. She unfolds it. Her breath catches.

TUCKER (CONT'D)
They called the house. About the scholarship.

PEGGY
Tucker.

TUCKER
You got in.

She sits down hard on the other stool. Reads the strip again. Her thumb pauses over the signature at the bottom.

Down in the lot, the cars dim their lights. A horn toots, impatient. Tucker watches her face.

PEGGY
(quietly)
Don't tell Dad.

TUCKER
He already knows.

PEGGY
What?

TUCKER
He read it first. Then he put it back.

She stares at him. Then at the window, where the first car's headlights flick on and off in a signal. She wipes her eyes with the back of her wrist. Stands. Presses the switch.

The projector clatters to life. A beam cuts across the lot and lands on the screen: a bright gold title card.

PEGGY (CONT'D)
(not looking at him)
Hand me the next reel.

Tucker slides it across. His fingers brush hers. She takes it, careful, and sets it beside the first.
